[92m21:25:06 - LiteLLM:DEBUG[0m: utils.py:400 - [92mlitellm.completion(model='vertex_ai/gemini-2.5-flash', api_key='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', api_base=None, api_version=None, timeout=None, drop_params=True, seed=None, messages=[{'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': '# **SYSTEM PROMPT — ANA-W1 (Circuit Synthesis Worker)**\n\nYou are **ANA-W1**, a **narrow, atomic circuit synthesis worker**.\n\nYou do **not** plan, interpret intent, arbitrate correctness, or manage workflow.\nYou execute **one task only**, then terminate.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Your Responsibility\n\nYour sole responsibility is to:\n\n> **Generate a complete and valid `tscircuit` `.tsx` circuit file from the provided inputs.**\n\nYou must:\n\n* faithfully construct the circuit described by the inputs\n* use only components available in the VHL Component Library\n* express all connectivity explicitly and accurately\n\nOnce the circuit code is generated, **your task is complete**.\n\nYou do **not**:\n\n* evaluate the circuit\n* validate correctness\n* reason about acceptance\n* retry or revise\n* explain errors\n* modify intent\n\n---\n\n## 2. Your Inputs\n\nYou are given the following inputs.\n\n### A. SCUD Document (Primary Intent Source)\n\nFrom SCUD, you must use **only** the following sections:\n\n* **Components Inventory**\n* **Connectivity & Signal Flow**\n* **VHL Component Library References**\n\nTreat SCUD as:\n\n* authoritative for **intent**\n* sufficient for **most** construction steps\n* already interpreted upstream\n\nYou must **not**:\n\n* reinterpret SCUD\n* resolve ambiguities conceptually\n* invent intent\n* modify or annotate SCUD\n\nSCUD may be **intentionally incomplete** with respect to exact wiring details.\n\n---\n\n### B. Schematic Image Corpus (Ground-Truth Structural Reference)\n\nYou are provided with a **schematic directory** containing:\n\n* one **reference schematic image** with annotated bounding boxes\n* multiple **named sub-circuit images**, each representing a concrete sub-circuit\n\nThese images represent the **ground-truth circuit realization**.\n\nYou must follow these rules:\n\n* SCUD defines **what the circuit is**\n* Schematic images define **how it is wired exactly**\n* If SCUD underspecifies or omits a connection, you may consult the relevant schematic image\n* If SCUD and schematic images appear to conflict:\n\n  * **Use the schematic image for wiring**\n  * **Use SCUD for intent and component identity**\n\nYou must **not**:\n\n* treat images as inspiration\n* reinterpret circuit intent from images\n* scan all images indiscriminately\n\nImage consultation is **selective and corrective**, not exploratory.\n\n---\n\n### C. VHL Component Library (Availability Constraint)\n\nYou may use **only components that are available** in the VHL Component Library.\n\nYou must:\n\n* instantiate/import components exactly as they exist in the library\n  (paths are managed by VHL; use relative imports)\n* assume all components listed in SCUD are available\n* respect pin names and pin mappings as defined by the library\n\nYou must **not**:\n\n* invent components\n* request new components\n* assume missing parts\n\n---\n\n## 3. Your Skills (How to Use Them)\n\nYou possess the following skills.\nThey are **tools**, not content.\n\n### A. tscircuit Operation Manual\n\nUse this skill to:\n\n* declare components\n* define nets and connections\n* structure a valid `.tsx` circuit file\n* follow correct syntax and conventions\n\n---\n\n### B. BFS-DC Circuit Building Strategy\n\nUse this strategy to:\n\n* build the circuit **incrementally**\n* start from core components\n* expand breadth-first\n* avoid dangling or implicit connections\n\n**This strategy remains mandatory even when schematic images are available.**\n\nYou must:\n\n* construct the circuit step by step\n* ensure each intermediate state is valid\n* consult schematic images **only when needed** to complete the current step\n\nYou must **not**:\n\n* generate the entire circuit in one pass\n* shortcut incremental construction using full schematic visibility\n\n---\n\n## 4. Output Requirements\n\nYou must produce:\n\n* **Exactly one `.tsx` file**\n* Containing:\n\n  * all component instantiations\n  * all nets\n  * all connections required to realize the circuit\n\nThe output must be:\n\n* complete\n* syntactically valid\n* self-contained\n\nDo **not**:\n\n* include explanations\n* include comments unrelated to the circuit\n* include analysis or reasoning text\n* include multiple variants\n\nYour final output must be **only the `.tsx` circuit code**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Operational Constraints (Very Important)\n\nStrictly follow these constraints:\n\n* Construct the `.tsx` file in **multiple passes**\n* First create the file with minimal valid structure\n* Update the file incrementally, adding components and connections\n* **Never** attempt to produce the entire file in a single pass\n\nAssume:\n\n* intent is already fixed\n* images are provided for completeness, not creativity\n* correctness will be judged externally\n\nYour job is **construction only**.\n\nOnce the circuit is produced, **stop**.\n\n---\n**NOTE**: Prioritize accuracy over everything(even speed). Take as many iterations, as much time as needed to ensure the output TSX file is 100% correct and complete. Make sure you refer to schematic images as needed to ensure correctness and clarify any ambiguities.\n\n<REPO_CONTEXT>\nThe following information has been included based on several files defined in user\'s repository.\nPlease follow them while working.\n\n\n[BEGIN context from [bfs-dc.md]]\n# **BFS-DC — Breadth-First Search with Degree Centrality**\n\n---\n\n## **Skill ID**\n\n`ANA-SKILL-BFS-DC`\n\n---\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nGuide ANA to **build and refine a circuit coherently across multiple agentic iterations** by:\n\n* Starting from **structurally important components or nets**\n* Expanding connectivity **breadth-first**\n* Growing the circuit **incrementally**, without destabilizing earlier structure\n* Strictly **do not** finish circuit building in one agentic iteration. Follow multiple passes.\nThis skill exists to control *how* ANA thinks, not *what* ANA computes.\n\n---\n\n## **Core Intuition**\n\n> **Some parts of a circuit matter more to global structure than others.\n> Start there, then expand outward gradually.**\n\n* “Degree Centrality” is used **qualitatively**, not numerically\n* “Breadth-First Search” describes **expansion style**, not traversal code\n\n---\n\n## **Mental Model (Lightweight)**\n\nANA maintains a **rough internal picture** of the circuit as it evolves:\n\n* Which components/nets are **structural anchors**\n* Which parts are **adjacent** to those anchors\n* Which parts are still **peripheral or undecided**\n\nThis picture is:\n\n* incomplete\n* approximate\n* continuously refined\n* never formalized as data structures\n\n---\n\n## **What “Degree Centrality” Means Here**\n\nANA uses *intuition*, not calculation.\n\nHigh-centrality elements are those that:\n\n* connect to many other things\n* define domains or boundaries\n* propagate constraints widely\n\nTypical examples:\n\n* Primary ICs\n* Power and ground rails\n* Shared buses\n* Isolation boundaries\n\nLow-centrality elements:\n\n* decoupling caps\n* pull-ups / pull-downs\n* EMI / termination parts\n\n---\n\n## **Skill Behavior Across Iterations**\n\n### **1. Initial Anchoring**\n\nAt the beginning of circuit construction, ANA:\n\n* Identifies a **small set of structurally important anchors**\n* Treats these as the **starting frontier**\n* Avoids deep detail elsewhere\n\nThis choice is **sticky** — it does not change lightly.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Breadth-First Expansion**\n\nANA expands outward from the anchors by:\n\n* Connecting **adjacent components or nets**\n* Handling one “layer” of proximity at a time\n* Prioritizing **interfaces and shared nets** over fine detail\n\nANA deliberately avoids:\n\n* finishing one IC completely in isolation\n* diving into secondary conditioning networks early\n\n---\n\n### **3. Incremental Graph Growth (Across Iterations)**\n\nAcross agentic iterations:\n\n* New components or connections are **added around the existing structure**\n* Earlier anchors remain stable reference points\n* The circuit “grows” rather than being reshaped wholesale\n\nImportantly:\n\n* ANA does **not** rebuild its mental model from scratch each iteration\n* Past structure constrains future reasoning\n\n---\n\n### **4. Late Peripheral Attachment**\n\nOnly after the main structure is stable does ANA:\n\n* attach low-impact components\n* refine conditioning networks\n* complete secondary details\n\nThese changes are assumed to have **local impact only**.\n\n---\n\n## **Interaction with Evaluation Failures**\n\nWhen evaluation fails:\n\n* ANA interprets errors relative to **where it is in the expansion**\n* Recent additions are questioned first\n* Core anchors are reconsidered **only if failure clearly implicates them**\n\nBFS-DC discourages global rewrites in response to local errors.\n\n---\n\n## **Operational Constraints**\n\nThis skill **must**:\n\n* respect SCUD as the source of intent\n* tolerate ambiguity without forcing resolution\n* preserve continuity across retries\n\nThis skill **must not**:\n\n* enforce strict ordering rules\n* require numerical scoring\n* behave like a fixed algorithm\n\n---\n\n## **Explicit Non-Goals**\n\nThis skill does **not**:\n\n* guarantee correctness\n* optimize performance\n* compute graph metrics\n* ensure convergence\n\nIt guarantees **reasoning stability**, not success.\n\n\n[END Context]\n\n[BEGIN context from [tscircuit_operation_manual.md]]\n# `tscircuit` Operational Guide\n\n## **Skill ID**\n\n`ANA-SKILL-TSCIRCUIT-OPERATION-GUIDE`\n\n## Circuit Assembly, Modules & Connectivity\n\n---\n\n## Overview\n\nThis guide describes **how to assemble circuits using `tscircuit`** by:\n\n* instantiating existing components\n* organizing them into modules\n* arranging schematic and PCB layouts\n* defining connectivity using nets and traces\n\nIt assumes that **all components referenced already exist** and are importable.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Circuit Structure\n\n### Root Container\n\nEvery circuit must be wrapped in a `<board />` element.\n\n```tsx\n<board width="100mm" height="80mm">\n  {/* circuit contents */}\n</board>\n```\n\n* `<board />` is the root of schematic and PCB generation\n* All components, nets, groups, and traces must be children of `<board />`\n\n---\n\n## 2. Using Existing Components\n\n### A. Built-in Primitives\n\n`tscircuit` provides globally available primitives that **do not require imports**.\n\nCommon examples:\n\n* `<resistor />`\n* `<capacitor />`\n* `<inductor />`\n* `<diode />`\n* `<led />`\n* `<jumper />`\n* `<pinheader />`\n\n#### Required Props for Passives\n\n| Element         | Required Props                     |\n| --------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `<resistor />`  | `name`, `resistance`, `footprint`  |\n| `<capacitor />` | `name`, `capacitance`, `footprint` |\n| `<inductor />`  | `name`, `inductance`, `footprint`  |\n| `<led />`       | `name`, `color`, `footprint`       |\n\nExample:\n\n```tsx\n<resistor\n  name="R1"\n  resistance="10k"\n  footprint="0603"\n/>\n```\n\n---\n\n### B. Imported Components\n\nComponents defined elsewhere (e.g., ICs or reusable blocks) may be imported and instantiated.\n\n```tsx\nimport { ControllerIC } from "./lib/ControllerIC"\n\n<ControllerIC name="U1" />\n```\n\n* Imported components are treated as black boxes\n* Pin names must be used exactly as exposed by the component\n\n---\n\n## 3. Nets\n\n### Declaring Nets\n\nNamed nets are used for shared electrical nodes.\n\n```tsx\n<net name="VCC" />\n<net name="GND" />\n```\n\n* Nets should be declared near the top of the `<board />`\n* Nets may be referenced anywhere within the board\n\n---\n\n### Connecting to Nets\n\nPins may be connected directly to nets.\n\n```tsx\n<trace from=".U1 > .VDD" to="net.VCC" />\n<trace from=".U1 > .GND" to="net.GND" />\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Connectivity\n\n### A. Point-to-Point Traces\n\nUse `<trace />` to connect two pins.\n\n```tsx\n<trace from=".U1 > .TX" to=".R1 > .pin1" />\n```\n\n* `from` and `to` use CSS-like selectors\n* Pin identifiers depend on the component definition\n\n---\n\n### B. Mixed Connectivity\n\nBoth nets and direct traces may coexist.\n\n```tsx\n<trace from=".C1 > .pin1" to="net.VCC" />\n<trace from=".C1 > .pin2" to="net.GND" />\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Modular Organization with `<group />`\n\n### Purpose\n\n`<group />` is a container for organizing related components and traces.\n\nIt may be used to:\n\n* cluster functionally related elements\n* simplify layout\n* move multiple elements together\n\n---\n\n### Auto-Layout Support\n\n`<group />` supports automatic layout for schematic and PCB.\n\n#### Schematic Layout Props\n\n* `schFlex`\n* `schFlexDirection="row" | "column"`\n* `schGap`\n\n#### PCB Layout Props\n\n* `pcbGrid`\n* `pcbGridCols`\n* `pcbGridGap`\n\n---\n\n### Example: Power Input Block\n\n```tsx\n<group\n  schFlex\n  schFlexDirection="row"\n  schGap="2mm"\n  pcbGrid\n  pcbGridCols={2}\n  pcbGridGap="1mm"\n>\n  <resistor name="R_Fuse" resistance="10" footprint="0603" />\n  <capacitor name="C_Bulk" capacitance="100uF" footprint="1206" />\n\n  <trace from=".R_Fuse > .pin2" to=".C_Bulk > .pos" />\n</group>\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Placement\n\n### Manual Placement\n\nComponents or groups may be positioned explicitly.\n\n* Schematic: `schX`, `schY`\n* PCB: `pcbX`, `pcbY`\n\n```tsx\n<capacitor\n  name="C1"\n  capacitance="100nF"\n  footprint="0402"\n  schX={10}\n  schY={5}\n  pcbX={12}\n  pcbY={8}\n/>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Placement Strategy\n\n* Manual coordinates are typically used at **top-level**\n* Auto-layout is preferred inside dense groups\n\n---\n\n## 7. Canonical Assembly Example\n\n```tsx\nimport { ControllerIC } from "./lib/ControllerIC"\n\nexport default () => {\n  return (\n    <board width="100mm" height="80mm">\n      <net name="VCC" />\n      <net name="GND" />\n\n      <ControllerIC name="U1" />\n\n      <capacitor\n        name="C_Decouple"\n        capacitance="100nF"\n        footprint="0402"\n      />\n\n      <trace from=".U1 > .VDD" to="net.VCC" />\n      <trace from=".U1 > .GND" to="net.GND" />\n      <trace from=".C_Decouple > .pin1" to="net.VCC" />\n      <trace from=".C_Decouple > .pin2" to="net.GND" />\n    </board>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n\n## 8. Built-in Elements Reference\n\n| Element                           | Description               |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| [`<battery />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/battery.md) | A power source that provides electrical energy through electrochemical reactions. |\n| [`<breakout />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/breakout.md) | A container used to guide the autorouter on where connections should exit a group. |\n| [`<breakoutpoint />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/breakoutpoint.md) | Marks the XY coordinate that the autorouter should use when connecting a net or pin inside a breakout. |\n| [`<cadassembly />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/cadassembly.md) | Used to put together the 3D models of a component when multiple models are used. |\n| [`<cadmodel />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/cadmodel.md) | Used to display a 3D model of a component. |\n| [`<capacitor />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/capacitor.md) | Stores electrical energy in an electric field, used for filtering, energy storage, and timing. |\n| [`<copperpour />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/copperpour.md) | Creates a copper pour (groundplane) connected to a specific net to improve signal integrity. |\n| [`<crystal />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/crystal.md) | Provides a stable clock signal essential for timing applications and microcontroller operations. |\n| [`<cutout />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/cutout.md) | Removes material from a board outline to add interior slots, mounting reliefs, or custom shapes. |\n| [`<diode />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/diode.md) | Semiconductor device that allows current to flow primarily in one direction. |\n| [`<footprint />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/footprint.md) | Defines PCB elements like plated holes or SMT pads for a component. |\n| [`<fuse />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/fuse.md) | A safety device that protects electrical circuits by interrupting current flow when it exceeds a threshold. |\n| [`<group />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/group.md) | Basic container element used for structural organization and layout of other elements. |\n| [`<hole />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/hole.md) | Used for mechanical mounting on the PCB; does not have conductive properties. |\n| [`<inductor />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/inductor.md) | Stores electrical energy in a magnetic field, used in filters, oscillators, and power supplies. |\n| [`<jumper />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/jumper.md) | Represents a small multi-pin connector, commonly a male or female header. Use `footprint` property to select the right number of pins(eg: `footprint="pinrow4"`) |\n| [`<led />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/led.md) | Light-emitting diode that emits light when forward current flows through it. |\n| [`<mosfet />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/mosfet.md) | A type of transistor used to control the flow of current in a circuit. |\n| [`<net />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/net.md) | Represents a group of connected traces, typically used for power buses and ground. |\n| [`<resistor />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/resistor.md) | A two-pin non-polar component that resists the flow of electricity. |\n| [`<solderjumper />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/solderjumper.md) | A tiny jumper made from exposed pads on the PCB that can be bridged or cut. |\n| [`<switch />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/switch.md) | A mechanical component used to connect or disconnect parts of a circuit. |\n| [`<testpoint />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/testpoint.md) | A designated location on a PCB for testing, debugging, and measuring electrical signals. |\n| [`<trace />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/trace.md) | Represents an electrical connection between two or more points in a circuit. |\n| [`<transistor />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/transistor.md) | A three-terminal semiconductor device used to amplify or switch electronic signals. |\n| [`<via />`](ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/tsci_built_in_elements/via.md) | A plated hole that electrically connects different layers of a PCB. |\n\nPlease refer to the individual markdown files for more details on each component.\n\n\n[END Context]\n\n</REPO_CONTEXT>'}], 'role': 'system'}, {'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': "Please process the SCUD file located at '/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana_worker/resources/bq79616_eval_board.scud'\nYou can refer to schematic images located at '/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana_worker/resources/schematic_images' for visual clarification. You can use file_editor tool to view these images as needed.\nRefer pin ana/resources/component_pin_mapping.md for component pin mapping information if needed.\nYou can use ana/output directory to store any output files you generate during the task."}], 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'thinking_blocks': [{'type': 'thinking', 'thinking': "Alright, here's the plan. I've got this SCUD document and some schematic images, and I need to whip up a `tscircuit.tsx` file. First things first: the SCUD document. Gotta understand the components, the connections, the whole damn shebang. No sense in building code on sand. Time to pull up the SCUD doc with the `file_editor` and get this project started right. Let's see what we're working with here...\n", 'signature': None}], 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_3d8b4b1bdd2d4a1fa15c6f52e62c', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'file_editor', 'arguments': '{"command": "view", "path": "/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana_worker/resources/bq79616_eval_board.scud", "security_risk": "LOW"}'}}]}, {'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': 'Here\'s the result of running `cat -n` on /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana_worker/resources/bq79616_eval_board.scud:\n     1\t# Circuit Overview & Functional Architecture\n     2\t\n     3\tThe schematic represents a high-cell-count Battery Management System (BMS) based on the **Texas Instruments BQ79616PAPQ1** Automotive Battery Monitor and Balancer. This IC is capable of monitoring up to 16 series cells.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe design includes:\n     6\t1.  **Main BMS IC (U1):** The BQ79616 handles cell voltage monitoring, internal cell balancing, and GPIO-based sensing.\n     7\t2.  **Cell Count Configuration:** A set of jumper resistors (currently DNP) allows the system to be configured for fewer than 16 cells by shorting unused upper channels (`CBxx`/`VCxx`) to the highest active potential.\n     8\t3.  **Temperature Sensing Interface:** A bank of 8 thermistor dividers powered by the IC\'s `TSREF` output. These connect to the IC\'s GPIO pins via a configuration header (J4).\n     9\t4.  **Power & Communication:** The IC is powered from the battery stack (`PWR` net) and generates its own internal rails (`AVDD`, `CVDD`, `DVDD`). It features a daisy-chainable communication interface (`COMH`/`COML`) and a local UART/SPI interface (`RX`/`TX`).\n    10\t5.  **Auxiliary Measurement Inputs:** The system includes two configurable paths for driving the `BBP` and `BBN` pins (Pins 64/63) of the BMS IC. These pins are typically used for auxiliary voltage measurements. The two options are:\n    11\t    *   **Bus Bar Measurement (DNP):** Measures the voltage difference between `BBP_CELL` and `BBN_CELL` via a differential RC filter.\n    12\t    *   **Current Sense Measurement (Active):** Measures the voltage from `SRP_S` and `SRN_S` (likely current sense shunt taps). This path is currently populated (direct connection).\n    13\t6.  **Communication Interface:**\n    14\t    *   **Isolated Interface:** A digital isolator (U2) provides a galvanically isolated UART and Fault interface between the BMS IC (High Voltage) and an external host/microcontroller (Low Voltage).\n    15\t    *   **Direct Interface:** A header is provided for direct, non-isolated connection to the BMS UART pins.\n    16\t\n    17\t7.  **NPN Power Supply:** An external NPN transistor circuit is provided to regulate the `LDOIN` supply for the BMS IC, reducing power dissipation within the IC. This circuit is powered from the main `PWR` rail.\n    18\t\n    19\t# Components Inventory\n    20\t\n    21\t**Integrated Circuits**\n    22\t*   **U1:** TI BQ79616PAPQ1 (16-Channel Battery Monitor)\n    23\t*   **U2:** TI ISO7342CQDWRQ1 (Digital Isolator)\n    24\t\n    25\t**Transistors**\n    26\t*   **Q1:** NPN Transistor (External LDO Pass Element). Active.\n    27\t*   **Q2:** NPN Transistor footprint. DNP (Do Not Populate).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Power & Decoupling**\n    30\t*   **Input Filter:** R5 (30.0Ω), C5 (10nF) on `BAT` pin.\n    31\t*   **NPN Supply:**\n    32\t    *   **Input Path:** `PWR` connects to Q1 Collector via series resistors R4 (200Ω) and R3 (100Ω).\n    33\t    *   **Input Filter:** C1 (0.22μF) on the Collector of Q1.\n    34\t    *   **Regulation:** Q1 Emitter drives `LDOIN`. Q1 Base is driven directly by `NPNB`.\n    35\t*   **Internal Regulators:**\n    36\t    *   **AVDD/CVDD/DVDD:** Decoupled by C6 (1μF), C9 (1μF), C7 (4.7μF), C8 (1μF).\n    37\t    *   **LDOIN:** C59 (0.1μF).\n    38\t    *   **NEG5V:** C3 (0.1μF).\n    39\t    *   **TSREF:** C2 (1μF).\n    40\t    *   **Isolator Power:** C57 (0.1μF) on `USB2ANY_3.3V`, C58 (0.1μF) on `CVDD_CO`.\n    41\t*   **Indicator:** Green LED D1 with R121 (1.0k) and Jumper J6 (Power Indicator).\n    42\t\n    43\t**Resistors (Configuration Jumpers)**\n    44\t*   **Type:** 0-ohm links\n    45\t*   **Status:** All currently marked DNP (Do Not Populate)\n    46\t*   **Designators:**\n    47\t    *   **CB Series:** R21, R22, R25, R26\n    48\t    *   **VC Series:** R23, R24, R27, R28\n    49\t    *   **Auxiliary Select:** R10, R13, R17 (DNP).\n    50\t\n    51\t**Auxiliary Measurement Components**\n    52\t*   **Bus Bar Filter:**\n    53\t    *   **Resistors:** R9, R12 (402Ω)\n    54\t    *   **Capacitor:** C10 (0.47μF)\n    55\t    *   **Test Points:** TP16, TP17\n    56\t*   **Current Sense Filter:**\n    57\t    *   **Capacitor:** C11 (0.47μF, DNP)\n    58\t    *   **Test Points:** TP18, TP19\n    59\t\n    60\t**Connectors & Jumpers**\n    61\t*   **J4:** 2x8 Header (GPIO Configuration). Connects GPIO pins to thermistor circuits.\n    62\t*   **J5:** 2-pin Header. Connects `TSREF` to `PULLUP`.\n    63\t*   **J17:** 2x5 Header (USB2ANY / MCU Interface).\n    64\t*   **J18:** 2-pin Header. Connects `CVDD` to `CVDD_CO` (Isolator Power).\n    65\t*   **J21:** 2-pin Header. Connects `RX_CO` (Isolator Output) to `RX` (BMS Input).\n    66\t*   **J1:** 2-pin Header. Connects `RX_C` (Direct Input) to `RX` (BMS Input).\n    67\t*   **J2:** 2-pin Header. Connects `NFAULT` to `NF_J` (Isolator Input).\n    68\t*   **J3:** 6-pin Header (Direct UART). Labeled "J3 Pin Description".\n    69\t\n    70\t**Test Points**\n    71\t*   **Power & Reference:**\n    72\t    *   TP1 (TSREF), TP2 (LDOIN), TP3 (NEG5V), TP4 (NPNB)\n    73\t    *   TP5 (CVDD), TP6 (AVDD), TP7 (REFHP), TP8 (BAT), TP9 (DVDD)\n    74\t*   **Ground:** TP13, TP14, TP15\n    75\t*   **Communication & Logic:** TP12 (RX), TP42 (TX), TP43 (NFAULT)\n    76\t*   **Auxiliary Sense:** TP10 (BBP), TP11 (BBN)\n    77\t\n    78\t**Temperature Sensing Components**\n    79\t*   **Pullup Resistors (10.0k):** R7, R8, R11, R14, R15, R16, R18, R19.\n    80\t*   **Thermistors (NTC):** RT1, RT2, RT3, RT4, RT5, RT6, RT7, RT8.\n    81\t*   **GPIO1 Filter:**\n    82\t    *   **R128:** 1.0k Resistor\n    83\t    *   **C60:** 1uF Capacitor\n    84\t    *   **D3:** 24V Dual Zener/TVS Diode\n    85\t*   **DNP Components:** R20 (100k), C12 (DNP) on RT8 channel.\n    86\t\n    87\t# Connectivity & Signal Flow\n    88\t\n    89\t**Main IC (U1) Connections:**\n    90\t*   **Cell Inputs:** `VC0` through `VC16` connect to the cell voltage sense lines.\n    91\t*   **Balancing:** `CB0` through `CB16` connect to the cell balance lines.\n    92\t*   **GPIOs:** `GPIO1` through `GPIO8` connect to the temperature sensing block (via J4).\n    93\t*   **Power Supply:**\n    94\t    *   `BAT` (Pin 1) is the high-voltage supply, filtered from the `PWR` net.\n    95\t    *   `AVDD`, `CVDD`, `DVDD` are internal regulator outputs tied together and decoupled to GND.\n    96\t    *   `TSREF` (Pin 51) provides the reference voltage for the thermistors.\n    97\t    *   `LDOIN` (Pin 47), `NEG5V` (Pin 44), `NPNB` (Pin 48), `REFHP` (Pin 37) are additional regulator/reference pins.\n    98\t    *   **NPN Supply (Optional):** `LDOIN` is powered via an external NPN (Q1). `PWR` -> R4 -> R3 -> Q1(C). Q1(E) -> `LDOIN`. Base is driven by `NPNB`. C1 filters the Collector input.\n    99\t*   **Communication:**\n   100\t    *   **Daisy Chain:** `COMHP`/`COMHN` (High side) and `COMLP`/`COMLN` (Low side) for stacking multiple BMS ICs.\n   101\t    *   **Local Host:** `RX` (Pin 52) and `TX` (Pin 53) for communication with a microcontroller.\n   102\t    *   **Fault:** `NFAULT` (Pin 62) is an active-low fault output.\n   103\t*   **Grounding:**\n   104\t    *   `AVSS`, `CVSS`, `DVSS`, `REFHM`, and `PAD` are tied to the local GND.\n   105\t    *   **System Grounding:** A note specifies that "GND is tied to CELL0 at connector via a thick trace," establishing the reference potential for the low-voltage side.\n   106\t\n   107\t**Communication Interface Details:**\n   108\t*   **Isolated Path (via U2):**\n   109\t    *   **TX (MCU -> BMS):** `USB2ANY_TX_3.3` (J17-7) -> U2 (INB->OUTB) -> `RX_CO` -> J21 -> `RX` (U1-52).\n   110\t    *   **RX (BMS -> MCU):** `TX` (U1-53) -> U2 (INC->OUTC) -> `USB2ANY_RX_3.3` (J17-8).\n   111\t    *   **Fault (BMS -> MCU):** `NFAULT` (U1-62) -> J2 -> `NF_J` -> U2 (IND->OUTD) -> `NFAULT_C` (J17-3).\n   112\t    *   **Power:** U2 Isolated side powered by `USB2ANY_3.3V`. BMS side powered by `CVDD` via J18.\n   113\t*   **Direct Path (Non-Isolated):**\n   114\t    *   **J3 (Direct UART Header):**\n   115\t        *   Pin 1: GND.\n   116\t        *   Pin 2: `NFAULT` (Schematic) / "FAULTn" (Text).\n   117\t        *   Pin 3: `RX_C` (Schematic) / "RX" (Text).\n   118\t        *   Pin 4: `TX` (Schematic) / "TX" (Text).\n   119\t        *   **Conflict:** The text description for J3 pins 4 and 5 ("4 RX", "5 TX") contradicts the schematic wires (Pin 4 is `TX`, Pin 3 is `RX_C`). The schematic wires are assumed correct: Pin 3 is Input (RX), Pin 4 is Output (TX).\n   120\t    *   `RX` (BMS) can be driven from `RX_C` (J3-3) via R119 (100Ω) and J1.\n   121\t    *   `TX` (BMS) connects directly to J3-4.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Auxiliary Measurement Inputs (BBP/BBN):**\n   124\t*   **Pins:** `BBP` (Pin 64) and `BBN` (Pin 63) on U1.\n   125\t*   **Path 1 (Bus Bar - DNP):**\n   126\t    *   Inputs: `BBP_CELL` and `BBN_CELL`.\n   127\t    *   Filtering: Differential RC filter formed by R9/R12 (402Ω) and C10 (0.47μF).\n   128\t    *   Connection: Connects to `BBP`/`BBN` via R10/R13 (0Ω). Currently DNP.\n   129\t*   **Path 2 (Current Sense - Active):**\n   130\t    *   Inputs: `SRP_S` and `SRN_S`.\n   131\t    *   Filtering: Optional C11 (0.47μF, DNP).\n   132\t    *   Connection: Directly wired to `BBP`/`BBN`. (Note: R17 is shown as DNP across the lines, likely a placeholder).\n   133\t    *   **Conflict Note:** The schematic shows both paths merging at the `BBP`/`BBN` nets. Since Path 1 resistors are DNP, Path 2 is the active source.\n   134\t\n   135\t**Cell Balance (CB) Configuration Path:**\n   136\t*   **R25** connects `CB12` to `CB13`\n   137\t*   **R21** connects `CB13` to `CB14`\n   138\t*   **R26** connects `CB14` to `CB15`\n   139\t*   **R22** connects `CB15` to `CB16`\n   140\t\n   141\t**Voltage Sense (VC) Configuration Path:**\n   142\t*   **R27** connects `VC12` to `VC13`\n   143\t*   **R23** connects `VC13` to `VC14`\n   144\t*   **R28** connects `VC14` to `VC15`\n   145\t*   **R24** connects `VC15` to `VC16`\n   146\t\n   147\tIn the current DNP state, all these lines (`CB12`-`CB16` and `VC12`-`VC16`) are electrically isolated from each other within this specific block.\n   148\t\n   149\t**Temperature Measurement Subsystem:**\n   150\t*   **Reference Power:** `TSREF` (Thermistor Reference) connects to the `PULLUP` rail via jumper **J5**.\n   151\t*   **Divider Network:** The `PULLUP` rail feeds 8 parallel voltage dividers. Each divider consists of a 10.0k top resistor (e.g., R7) and a bottom NTC thermistor (e.g., RT1) to GND.\n   152\t*   **Measurement Points:** The midpoint of each divider is labeled `GPIOx_R` (e.g., `GPIO8_R`, `GPIO7_R`... `GPIO1_R`).\n   153\t*   **GPIO Interface (J4):**\n   154\t    *   **J4** allows connecting the BMS IC\'s `GPIOx` pins (odd pins 1-15) to the measurement points.\n   155\t    *   **Channels 2-8:** `GPIOx` connects directly to `GPIOx_R` when the jumper is placed.\n   156\t    *   **Channel 1:** Unlike channels 2-8, Channel 1 includes an intermediate filter stage. The divider node `GPIO1_R` (with C60 and D3) connects via R128 (1k) to `GPIO1_C`. `GPIO1_C` then connects to J4 Pin 2. Thus, the BMS pin `GPIO1` measures the filtered divider voltage through a 1k series resistor.\n   157\t    *   **TP44** provides a test point on the `GPIO1_R` net (the divider node).\n   158\t\n   159\t\n   160\t# Uncertainties, Assumptions & Confidence\n   161\t\n   162\t*   **Assumption:** The numbers 12 through 16 in the configuration block represent the cell index in the stack.\n   163\t*   **Uncertainty:** The destination of the vertical lines (`CBxx`/`VCxx`) is now confirmed to be U1 on one side, but the connection to the actual battery connector/filter components is not yet visible.\n   164\t*   **Uncertainty:** Why only GPIO1 has the explicit filter/protection circuit (R128, C60, D3) shown.\n   165\t*   **Uncertainty:** The exact nature of D3 (24V). It appears to be a bidirectional TVS or Zener pair for overvoltage protection.\n   166\t*   **Resolved:** The main IC is identified as TI BQ79616PAPQ1.\n   167\t*   **Resolved:** `CB` and `VC` lines connect to the main IC.\n   168\t*   **Resolved:** `TSREF` source is U1 Pin 51.\n   169\t*   **Uncertainty:** The labels "BBN_CELL" and "BBP_CELL" in the Bus Bar section appear swapped relative to the port symbols. I have assumed the port symbols are correct.\n   170\t*   **Uncertainty:** The exact nature of `SRP_S`/`SRN_S`. They are likely "Sense Resistor Positive/Negative Sense" lines, but it is unclear if they come from a raw shunt or an amplifier. Given the direct connection to the BMS IC\'s auxiliary ADC inputs, they are likely raw shunt voltages (or slightly filtered), assuming the BMS IC has a suitable input range (usually +/- 200mV or similar for current sense).\n   171\t*   **Uncertainty:** R17 is DNP and placed across the `SRP_S`/`SRN_S` lines. It might be a placeholder for a differential filter resistor or a zero-ohm short for testing.\n   172\t*   **Uncertainty:** The purpose of U2 `INA` (Pin 3) being tied to GND via R123. It might be an unused channel or a configuration pin.\n   173\t*   **Uncertainty:** U2 `OUTA` (Pin 14) appears unconnected.\n   174\t*   **Resolved:** Q1 is active, Q2 is DNP. The NPN supply circuit is populated.\n   175\t*   **Conflict:** J3 pinout text vs schematic wires. Text says Pin 5 is TX, Pin 4 is RX. Schematic wires show Pin 4 is TX, Pin 3 is RX_C. Schematic wires are assumed correct.\n   176\t\n   177\t# VHL Component Library References\n   178\t\n   179\t| Designator | Component Name |\n   180\t|------------|----------------|\n   181\t| U1 | BQ79616PAPR |\n   182\t| U2 | ISO7342FCQDWRQ1 |\n   183\t| Q1 | MMBT3904LT1G |\n   184\t| D1 | 150060GS75000 |\n   185\t| D3 | BZX84C24 |\n   186\t| RT1-RT8 | NCP18XH103F03RB |\n   187\t\n   188\t\n'}], 'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_3d8b4b1bdd2d4a1fa15c6f52e62c', 'name': 'file_editor'}, {'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': "I've completed the implementation of the NPN power supply circuit, connecting Q1, R3, R4, and C1 according to the SCUD.\n\nNext, I will implement the Cell Balance (CB) Configuration Path and the Voltage Sense (VC) Configuration Path. These involve a set of 0-ohm resistors (R21, R22, R25, R26 for CB and R23, R24, R27, R28 for VC) that are currently DNP (Do Not Populate). Even though they are DNP, their connections are specified. I will instantiate them and define their connections.\n\nFrom SCUD:\n**Cell Balance (CB) Configuration Path:**\n*   `R25` connects `CB12` to `CB13`\n*   `R21` connects `CB13` to `CB14`\n*   `R26` connects `CB14` to `CB15`\n*   `R22` connects `CB15` to `CB16`\n\n**Voltage Sense (VC) Configuration Path:**\n*   `R27` connects `VC12` to `VC13`\n*   `R23` connects `VC13` to `VC14`\n*   `R28` connects `VC14` to `VC15`\n*   `R24` connects `VC15` to `VC16`\n\nI will also connect the respective `CBx