J1`, `J2`, and `J3`.\n\nAfter that, I'll connect U1, likely the BMS IC, UART and NFAULT pins to the appropriate nets. This involves careful routing to establish the data flow.\n\nThen, I'll flesh out the isolated path: The `USB2ANY_TX_3_3` signal from J17 will go into U2's input. U2's output will route via `RX_CO` and `J21` and then be connected to `RX_BMS`. The transmit signal from the BMS will connect to U2's input, and U2's output will be connected to the `USB2ANY_RX_3_3` pin. Similarly, the NFAULT signals will be routed via the isolated path. Power and ground connections for U2 need to be implemented as well. I'll need to use `CVDD` from the IC, route it through `J18` and `U2.VDD`. The isolated ground `GND` will be routed accordingly.\n\nThen, the direct path. The receive signal, `RX_C`, from the direct path header, will need to be routed to `J1` and then to `RX_BMS`. The BMS's transmit signal will be directly connected to the appropriate pin on `J3`, the direct connection header. Ground and the fault signal will also be connected.\n\nFinally, I'll add test points for debugging: `TP12`, `TP42`, and `TP43` will be used to monitor the RX, TX, and NFAULT signals, which is a standard procedure in my design process. \n", 'signature': None}], 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_831ea25bf78f4dd6bc7652cee608', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'file_editor', 'arguments': '{"new_str": "      <trace from=\\"net.GPIO1_R\\" to=\\".TP44 > .pin\\" />\\n\\n      {/* New Nets for Communication Interface */}\\n      <net name=\\"USB2ANY_TX_3_3\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"USB2ANY_RX_3_3\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"NFAULT_C\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"RX_CO\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"TX_CO\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"NF_J\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"RX_C\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"TX_BMS\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"RX_BMS\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"NFAULT_BMS\\" />\\n\\n      {/* Connectors & Jumpers */}\\n      <jumper name=\\"J17\\" footprint=\\"pinheader-2x5\\" />\\n      <jumper name