rent Sense Filter (C11, R17).\n    *   Test Points (TP16, TP17, TP18, TP19).\n    *   Connect U1\'s BBP/BBN pins.\n6.  **Implement Temperature Measurement Subsystem:**\n    *   J5 (TSREF to PULLUP).\n    *   Pullup Resistors (R7, R8, R11, R14, R15, R16, R18, R19).\n    *   Thermistors (RT1-RT8).\n    *   GPIO1 Filter (R128, C60, D3).\n    *   J4 (GPIO Configuration Header).\n    *   DNP Components (R20, C12).\n    *   Test Point TP44.\n    *   Connect U1\'s GPIO pins.\n7.  **Implement Communication Interface:**\n    *   Isolated Path (U2, J17, J18, J21, J2).\n    *   Direct Path (J3, J1).\n    *   Test Points (TP12, TP42, TP43).\n    *   Connect U1\'s RX, TX, NFAULT pins.\n\nI will proceed incrementally, starting with the file initialization and then adding components and connections section by section. I\'ll pay specific attention to the jumpers and U2 connections during their respective sections.'}], 'role': 'assistant', 'thinking_blocks': [{'type': 'thinking', 'thinking': 'Alright, let\'s get this done. I\'m going to create a new `bq79616_circuit.tsx` file, clean and fresh. The last version had some unnecessary trace definitions, so this time I\'ll be extra careful, especially with the `ISO7342 Connections` and `Jumper Implementation`, just as the feedback suggested. I\'ll have the schematics open for easy reference.\n\nHere\'s the plan: First, I\'ll get the basic board structure up and running – the imports, the global nets declared. Then, I\'ll get all the components down, according to the "Components Inventory" section, including U1, U2, Q1, and all the resistors, capacitors, and jumpers.\n\nNext, I\'ll focus on power and decoupling, making sure the input filters, the NPN supply, and all the internal regulator and isolator decoupling capacitors are correctly placed, along with the power indicator.\n\nAfter that, the cell count configuration will be next, then I\'ll make the auxiliary measurement inputs and connect the components to the correct VC and CB pins.\n\nThe temperature measurement subsystem is coming up after that, with pull-up resistors, thermistors, and the GPIO1 filter, and finally connecting the GPIO pins.\n\nLastly, the communication interface. That means the isolated path with U2, the direct path, and the test points, ensuring everything\'s connected to U1\'s RX, TX, and NFAULT pins.\n\nI\'ll tackle this step-by-step, section by section. The jumpers and U2 connections will get extra attention when I get to their respective sections. Let\'s get to work.\n', 'signature': None}], 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_5d86b08cce2a4c30a7b3a54aa8ed', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'file_editor', 'arguments': '{"path": "/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/output/bq79616_circuit.tsx", "security_risk": "LOW", "file_text": "import { BQ79616PAPR } from \\"./components/BQ79616PAPR\\";\\nimport { ISO7342FCQDWRQ1 } from \\"./components/ISO7342FCQDWRQ1\\";\\nimport { MMBT3904LT1G } from \\"./components/MMBT3904LT1G\\";\\nimport { NCP18XH103F03RB } from \\"./components/NCP18XH103F03RB\\";\\nimport { BZX84C24 } from \\"./components/BZX84C24\\";\\n\\nexport default () => {\\n  return (\\n    <board width=\\"100mm\\" height=\\"80mm\\">\\n      {/* Nets */}\\n      <net name=\\"VCC\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"GND\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"PWR\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"NPNB\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"LDOIN\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"AVDD\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"CVDD\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"DVDD\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"NEG5V\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"TSREF\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"REFHP\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"BAT\\" />\\n\\n      {/* Cell Voltage and Balance Nets */}\\n      <net name=\\"VC0\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"VC1\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"VC2\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"VC3\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"VC4\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"VC5\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"VC6\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"VC7\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"VC8\\" />\\n      <net name=\\"VC9\\" />\\n      <net