            "text": "Okay, let's get this straight. Based on the SCUD description of the J4 connector, I made a mistake in the initial mapping of the U1 GPIOs to J4. The description clearly states that J4's odd pins (1-15) connect to the GPIOx pins. This means J4.pin1 connects to U1.GPIO1, J4.pin3 connects to U1.GPIO2, and so on.  I corrected this mapping in the previous step.\n\nNow, I need to make sure the *other* side of the jumper, connecting to the measurement points, is also correct. The SCUD indicates that J4.pin2 connects to `GPIO1_C`, J4.pin4 to `GPIO2_R`, and so on. The previously added connections starting with `trace from=\"net.GPIO1_C\" to=\".J4 \u003e .pin2\" /\u003e`, seem correct for connecting the measurement points to the even pins of J4. \n\nSo, in summary, for J4, we have: U1.GPIO1 -\u003e J4.pin1, GPIO1_C -\u003e J4.pin2, U1.GPIO2 -\u003e J4.pin3, GPIO2_R -\u003e J4.pin4, etc.  This makes perfect sense: a jumper would short the odd and even pins of J4, connecting a GPIO pin to its corresponding measurement point, which is exactly how these headers are designed to work.\n\nMoving on, I've reviewed the ISO7342 connections (U2). They look spot-on. Power, data (TX/RX), and fault lines are correctly routed through U2 and the relevant jumpers, including the `R123` on U2.INA.\n\nFinally, I carefully checked *all* of the jumper implementations (J1, J2, J3, J4, J5, J6, J17, J18, J21), and their connections and the overall structure. All look correct and aligned with the SCUD, down to the pin numbers on the 2x5 header (J17). There are no apparent errors or redundancies.\n\nTime to do a final sweep of the entire file to check for any missed issues, especially that `TP44` trace duplication I saw earlier.\n",
