                             GPIO-based sensing.\n     7\t2.  **Cell Count Configuration:** A set of jumper resistors                   
                             (currently DNP) allows the system to be configured for fewer than 16 cells by shorting unused              
                             upper channels (`CBxx`/`VCxx`) to the highest active potential.\n     8\t3.  **Temperature                 
                             Sensing Interface:** A bank of 8 thermistor dividers powered by the IC's `TSREF` output. These             
                             connect to the IC's GPIO pins via a configuration header (J4).\n     9\t4.  **Power &                      
                             Communication:** The IC is powered from the battery stack (`PWR` net) and generates its own                
                             internal rails (`AVDD`, `CVDD`, `DVDD`). It features a daisy-chainable communication interface             
                             (`COMH`/`COML`) and a local UART/SPI interface (`RX`/`TX`).\n    10\t5.  **Auxiliary                       
                             Measurement Inputs:** The system includes two configurable paths for driving the `BBP` and                 
                             `BBN` pins (Pins 64/63) of the BMS IC. These pins are typically used for auxiliary voltage                 
                             measurements. The two options are:\n    11\t    *   **Bus Bar Measurement (DNP):** Measures                
                             the voltage difference between `BBP_CELL` and `BBN_CELL` via a differential RC filter.\n                   
                             12\t    *   **Current Sense Measurement (Active):** Measures the voltage from `SRP_S` and                  
                             `SRN_S` (likely current sense shunt taps). This path is currently populated (direct                        
                             connection).\n    13\t6.  **Communication Interface:**\n    14\t    *   **Isolated                         
                             Interface:** A digital isolator (U2) provides a galvanically isolated UART and Fault interface             
                             between the BMS IC (High Voltage) and an external host/microcontroller (Low Voltage).\n                    
                             15\t    *   **Direct Interface:** A header is provided for direct, non-isolated connection to              
                             the BMS UART pins.\n    16\t\n    17\t7.  **NPN Power Supply:** An external NPN transistor                 
                             circuit is provided to regulate the `LDOIN` supply for the BMS IC, reducing power dissipation              
